1
00:00:24,532 --> 00:00:32,197
[SPEAKER_00]: And it's about ten perceptions that need to be developed.

2
00:00:37,941 --> 00:00:40,483
[SPEAKER_00]: The first one is...

3
00:00:40,503 --> 00:00:42,424
[SPEAKER_00]: I'll just read that first section.

4
00:00:42,684 --> 00:00:46,527
[SPEAKER_00]: What is the perception of anicca that needs to be developed?

5
00:00:47,347 --> 00:00:49,829
[SPEAKER_00]: Here, a bhikkhu, having gone to the forest, to the

6
00:00:55,832 --> 00:00:56,392
[SPEAKER_00]: Anitta is anicca.

7
00:00:56,673 --> 00:00:57,754
[SPEAKER_00]: Feeling is anicca.

8
00:00:58,374 --> 00:00:59,575
[SPEAKER_00]: Perception is anicca.

9
00:01:00,315 --> 00:01:02,097
[SPEAKER_00]: Saṅkharas are anicca.

10
00:01:02,797 --> 00:01:04,078
[SPEAKER_00]: Consciousness is anicca.

11
00:01:05,179 --> 00:01:11,424
[SPEAKER_00]: He lives as one who sees anicca in the right way, in these five assumed aggregates.

12
00:01:14,746 --> 00:01:22,152
[SPEAKER_00]: So, just to... the developing these perceptions is meditation.

13
00:01:23,366 --> 00:01:30,607
[SPEAKER_01]: Yes, that begins by going to an empty forest, an empty hut, physically removed from people.

14
00:01:32,308 --> 00:01:32,728
[SPEAKER_01]: And obviously...

15
00:01:32,748 --> 00:01:33,528
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a prerequisite.

16
00:01:33,828 --> 00:01:47,671
[SPEAKER_01]: And prerequisite for succeeding in living alone like that is development in virtue, establishment in virtue, accomplishment in virtue, sense restraint and guarding of the sense doors.

17
00:01:50,639 --> 00:01:51,580
[SPEAKER_01]: Watchfulness and so on.

18
00:01:52,280 --> 00:01:54,962
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's where the meditation begins.

19
00:01:54,982 --> 00:01:56,002
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's with your virtue.

20
00:01:57,783 --> 00:02:06,168
[SPEAKER_01]: Then, the next step, when you're thoroughly established in your precepts and guarding with the sense doors, is that going to an empty forest hut.

21
00:02:06,749 --> 00:02:08,970
[SPEAKER_01]: That's when these perceptions become clearer.

22
00:02:10,151 --> 00:02:12,292
[SPEAKER_01]: When you're physically removed from company.

23
00:02:12,432 --> 00:02:13,833
[SPEAKER_00]: And can then be developed.

24
00:02:14,033 --> 00:02:15,454
[SPEAKER_00]: Then you can start meditating, so to speak.

25
00:02:15,734 --> 00:02:15,994
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

26
00:02:16,174 --> 00:02:19,216
[SPEAKER_01]: This is prerequisite for jhāna, prerequisite for any of these perceptions.

27
00:02:24,672 --> 00:02:28,933
[SPEAKER_01]: Empty forest, empty heart, dwelling away.

28
00:02:29,033 --> 00:02:30,993
[SPEAKER_01]: That's not optional.

29
00:02:31,173 --> 00:02:32,894
[SPEAKER_00]: Secluded from unwholesome states.

30
00:02:32,974 --> 00:02:33,434
[SPEAKER_00]: Secluded from?

31
00:02:33,454 --> 00:02:34,674
[SPEAKER_01]: Secluded from unwholesome states.

32
00:02:34,854 --> 00:02:35,274
[SPEAKER_01]: Company.

33
00:02:35,654 --> 00:02:36,054
[SPEAKER_01]: Exactly.

34
00:02:36,094 --> 00:02:43,916
[SPEAKER_01]: But physically, you have to have been removed from company, from the possibility of company, from the immediate possibility of company.

35
00:02:44,936 --> 00:02:51,817
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, I might be alone in the room, but if somebody is next door to me, they can come and knock at any given time,

36
00:02:52,805 --> 00:02:53,125
[SPEAKER_01]: Or, eh...

37
00:02:53,185 --> 00:02:56,408
[SPEAKER_01]: I could hear them at any given time, or they could call me at any given time.

38
00:02:56,668 --> 00:02:57,569
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you're not really alone.

39
00:02:58,169 --> 00:02:59,730
[SPEAKER_01]: That possibility is looming over.

40
00:03:00,711 --> 00:03:06,115
[SPEAKER_01]: But when you're secluded in a... in a... in a way that the suttas describe it, it's like, okay, now you're secluded.

41
00:03:06,155 --> 00:03:12,740
[SPEAKER_01]: Now you know that, you know, at least until tomorrow, when you get back to the village or something, there will be no people here.

42
00:03:13,341 --> 00:03:14,021
[SPEAKER_01]: You are alone.

43
00:03:14,962 --> 00:03:16,363
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you use that certain

44
00:03:25,214 --> 00:03:27,956
[SPEAKER_01]: This is the default nature of being alone.

45
00:03:28,897 --> 00:03:31,639
[SPEAKER_01]: The five aggregates being there in the world on their own.

46
00:03:32,460 --> 00:03:35,882
[SPEAKER_01]: Empty of owner, empty of controller, empty of the master.

47
00:03:37,463 --> 00:03:41,907
[SPEAKER_01]: And so, you use that certain current basis of actual, factual seclusion.

48
00:03:41,947 --> 00:03:44,369
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, this room is empty of others, as the Buddha says.

49
00:03:45,289 --> 00:03:48,492
[SPEAKER_01]: So, is these aggregates are empty of selves or something like that, similar.

50
00:03:50,486 --> 00:04:01,297
[SPEAKER_00]: But just to... in terms of anicca, so being in seclusion, being withdrawn from unwholesome states, that perception becomes clear.

51
00:04:03,078 --> 00:04:06,902
[SPEAKER_00]: So you're not... you're not creating anicca.

52
00:04:07,262 --> 00:04:08,163
[SPEAKER_01]: No, not fabricating.

53
00:04:08,183 --> 00:04:10,365
[SPEAKER_00]: You're not fabricating, you're not imagining it.

54
00:04:10,506 --> 00:04:15,030
[SPEAKER_01]: Expanding your mind in that peripheral sense, where you notice more and more

55
00:04:15,568 --> 00:04:42,320
[SPEAKER_01]: Until you notice, yeah, that any of the aggregates sitting there on its own, peripherally, enduring, because of which you have this experience of being alone in the forest or an empty hut at the moment, and then the characteristics of that, of that aggregate, or of the five aggregates, or of the experience as a whole, or of that body there, whichever is understood peripherally, will be seen as, yeah, impersonal, unownable, given, existing on its own.

56
00:04:43,301 --> 00:04:43,961
[SPEAKER_01]: for what it is.

57
00:04:44,081 --> 00:04:44,302
[SPEAKER_01]: Right.

58
00:04:44,702 --> 00:04:47,003
[SPEAKER_01]: And that's all forms of anicca, seeing anicca.

59
00:04:48,424 --> 00:04:54,227
[SPEAKER_00]: So, you're able then to see the nature of this experience.

60
00:04:54,267 --> 00:04:54,467
[SPEAKER_01]: Yeah.

61
00:04:55,048 --> 00:05:04,013
[SPEAKER_00]: So, why is that nature of experience, that perception of anicca, not obvious to us, not obvious to everyone?

62
00:05:05,494 --> 00:05:07,675
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, because people, people's minds,

63
00:05:15,516 --> 00:05:24,561
[SPEAKER_01]: Because they're too absorbed with the pull of sensuality, with the avoidance of pain, with the gratuitous perpetuation of ownership.

64
00:05:25,761 --> 00:05:36,507
[SPEAKER_01]: So, clouding, clouding the perspective, or just, you know, as the Buddha would compare, like, you know, living immersed in a swamp and water.

65
00:05:37,247 --> 00:05:41,309
[SPEAKER_01]: So, for as long as the lifestyle and livelihood doesn't change,

66
00:05:43,710 --> 00:05:47,571
[SPEAKER_01]: whereby it stops perpetuating these gratuitous assumptions.

67
00:05:48,312 --> 00:05:53,494
[SPEAKER_01]: You can't see them, but you would see if you don't have those assumptions.

68
00:05:56,916 --> 00:06:02,618
[SPEAKER_01]: So that's why I said many times that the practice of the dhamma is not something you can do on top of your life, like whatever.

69
00:06:03,799 --> 00:06:10,622
[SPEAKER_01]: The practice of dhamma means restructuring your life, so it matches the principles of

70
00:06:11,958 --> 00:06:14,239
[SPEAKER_01]: nature of the five aggregates, before it's too late.

71
00:06:15,540 --> 00:06:23,163
[SPEAKER_01]: And the importance for matching those principles is so that you can develop this passion and remove the gratuitous ownership of those very same aggregates.

72
00:06:25,123 --> 00:06:33,327
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's not... it's not some like a revelation you can get on top of your currently, sort of, you know, ordinary life, and then you just suddenly see this.

73
00:06:33,407 --> 00:06:33,747
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no.

74
00:06:34,307 --> 00:06:36,408
[SPEAKER_01]: You need to start undoing everything else that

75
00:06:38,406 --> 00:06:39,586
[SPEAKER_01]: Removing the cover-ups.

76
00:06:39,806 --> 00:06:48,568
[SPEAKER_01]: And the entire life, the entire human life, is one massive cover-up of non-ownership, of anicca, of fear, of anxiety, of death, and everything else.

77
00:06:48,628 --> 00:06:50,249
[SPEAKER_01]: It's a giant cover-up.

78
00:06:50,289 --> 00:06:53,630
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why people are addicted and passionately dependent upon that life.

79
00:06:53,710 --> 00:06:58,671
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if they look, you know, what the alternative is, it's frightening.

80
00:06:58,791 --> 00:07:00,651
[SPEAKER_01]: It says, oh, what if I'm not in control?

81
00:07:01,431 --> 00:07:04,432
[SPEAKER_01]: What if, you know, being subject to death is something that people

82
00:07:10,660 --> 00:07:19,228
[SPEAKER_01]: And it's going to be very unpleasant, because everything that is there to me, and that I depend upon, will cease, will be taken away.

83
00:07:21,250 --> 00:07:22,711
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah, I don't want to know that.

84
00:07:23,012 --> 00:07:24,012
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yeah, exactly.

85
00:07:24,053 --> 00:07:32,100
[SPEAKER_01]: So, it's too much to deal with, so it's better for me to basically not acknowledge it, and not want to know about it, yeah.

86
00:07:33,301 --> 00:07:34,142
[SPEAKER_01]: Enjoy life.

87
00:07:34,562 --> 00:07:43,708
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, and then, you know, you might not necessarily enjoy life, but by choosing to ignore this, then you're already choosing the direction of trying to enjoy life and pretend there is no problem underneath.

88
00:07:49,452 --> 00:08:03,361
[SPEAKER_00]: So that, yeah, you can understand it intellectually, anicca, you can get the dictionary definition, and you can understand it on that level of information.

89
00:08:04,572 --> 00:08:09,413
[SPEAKER_00]: But if you don't change your life, you'll never actually see it or discern it.

90
00:08:09,794 --> 00:08:10,754
[SPEAKER_01]: No, no, no.

91
00:08:11,554 --> 00:08:12,214
[SPEAKER_00]: The actual nature.

92
00:08:12,234 --> 00:08:23,018
[SPEAKER_01]: No, that's why, you know, the physical actions need to be purified, the livelihood needs to be purified, the whole, like, speech needs to be purified, the habits need to be, you know, purified, abandoned, whatever else.

93
00:08:23,798 --> 00:08:28,459
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, all these things you read in the suttas, you'll have a basis to see them within your experience.

94
00:08:29,339 --> 00:08:31,000
[SPEAKER_01]: Without it, it's just an abstract theory.

95
00:08:39,747 --> 00:08:41,829
[SPEAKER_01]: You're actually internally cultivating.

96
00:08:45,311 --> 00:08:45,692
[SPEAKER_00]: Yeah.

97
00:08:47,373 --> 00:09:03,305
[SPEAKER_00]: And in terms of that, in terms of translation, so, nītya, usually translated as impermanence, you know, gives that sense of just, you know, it won't be around for very long, it will change eventually, but it's not deep enough.

98
00:09:03,906 --> 00:09:05,187
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not deep enough because it's

99
00:09:09,777 --> 00:09:12,018
[SPEAKER_01]: Or at least it has become too impersonal.

100
00:09:13,118 --> 00:09:17,399
[SPEAKER_01]: So, when you say impermanence, you think it's any random impermanence of any random thing.

101
00:09:18,180 --> 00:09:19,600
[SPEAKER_01]: That's what anicca is, but it's not.

102
00:09:20,560 --> 00:09:26,842
[SPEAKER_01]: Nicca, remember that we found that in original Sanskrit, nitya means dear, personal, intimate.

103
00:09:28,143 --> 00:09:32,004
[SPEAKER_01]: So, the whole nicca, the whole sense of permanence, durability,

104
00:09:32,680 --> 00:09:37,063
[SPEAKER_01]: It's bound with that intimacy, it's bound with that ownership, with that which is dear to you.

105
00:09:37,804 --> 00:09:40,266
[SPEAKER_01]: So, anicca should be seen on that level.

106
00:09:41,106 --> 00:10:01,922
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why I'm saying, if you want to practically see anicca, you need to find things that are dear to you, things that you would not remain sort of indifferent, if they change, those things, if they go away, or if they change, and then contemplate them going away and changing, to see how much emotionally you're dependent on that, and how much you're refusing to accept the reality that they are subject to change.

107
00:10:02,657 --> 00:10:03,958
[SPEAKER_01]: They are subject to anicca.

108
00:10:04,278 --> 00:10:04,638
[SPEAKER_01]: That's it.

109
00:10:08,279 --> 00:10:16,443
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why translating it as impermanence, I mean, it's fine if if you explain where that impermanence is found.

110
00:10:17,604 --> 00:10:24,227
[SPEAKER_01]: But if, you know, it's just impermanence of everything and nothing, that's not anicca at all, because it's not personal enough.

111
00:10:24,627 --> 00:10:29,129
[SPEAKER_00]: I also like the translation of circumstantial.

112
00:10:30,609 --> 00:10:31,570
[SPEAKER_01]: Well, again, even that.

113
00:10:32,356 --> 00:10:36,478
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, whichever translation you choose, I think, you would have to explain it, really, yeah.

114
00:10:37,298 --> 00:10:46,161
[SPEAKER_01]: Because circumstantial people can then interpret, could possibly interpret as, oh, this is circumstantial, thus it's on my control, thus it's on my responsibility, thus I can dismiss it.

115
00:10:47,602 --> 00:10:50,823
[SPEAKER_01]: But your attachment isn't your responsibility, is in your control.

116
00:10:51,063 --> 00:10:52,224
[SPEAKER_01]: Not directly, indirectly.

117
00:10:52,864 --> 00:10:55,405
[SPEAKER_01]: It's because of the way you regard things, you are attached to them.

118
00:10:55,745 --> 00:10:57,786
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are responsible for it.

119
00:10:58,626 --> 00:11:00,607
[SPEAKER_01]: You know, if you say, oh, I keep, I don't know,

120
00:11:02,330 --> 00:11:05,912
[SPEAKER_01]: Drinking cold water non-stop and having headaches as a result of it.

121
00:11:06,893 --> 00:11:14,717
[SPEAKER_01]: So, yes, you're not directly causing yourself headaches, but if you stop drinking the water that is very cold, your headaches will go away.

122
00:11:16,078 --> 00:11:18,119
[SPEAKER_01]: So, you are responsible for them, indirectly.

123
00:11:19,780 --> 00:11:23,102
[SPEAKER_01]: You're not drinking water to get headaches, but they are the result.

124
00:11:24,022 --> 00:11:32,067
[SPEAKER_01]: So, your attachment to things that are dear to you, yeah, you might want not to be attached, but you keep doing things that attachment is the result of.

125
00:11:33,340 --> 00:11:35,182
[SPEAKER_01]: That's why I keep saying it's the symptoms of the problem.

126
00:11:35,202 --> 00:11:54,097
[SPEAKER_01]: And the problem is basically you acting out of passion, aversion and distraction, not taking responsibility on the level of choice, and certainly not having developed virtue sufficiently enough, which is like, you know, a certain basis that would already start challenging your notion of, well, self and ownership and things that are dear to you.

127
00:11:54,798 --> 00:11:56,379
[SPEAKER_01]: Even just keeping the mere precepts.

128
00:12:03,118 --> 00:12:09,782
[SPEAKER_00]: So, if we think about the matter is anicca, matter or feeling is anicca.

129
00:12:12,784 --> 00:12:18,567
[SPEAKER_00]: I would say matter is inaccessible.

130
00:12:19,748 --> 00:12:27,313
[SPEAKER_00]: It's arisen circumstantially through elements and things that I have no access to.

131
00:12:28,609 --> 00:12:36,056
[SPEAKER_01]: But again, whether that's done as an explanation, or as... or is that done as a description of what you're peripherally seeing?

132
00:12:37,597 --> 00:12:40,700
[SPEAKER_01]: Correctly, through mindfulness of the body, peripherally established.

133
00:12:41,601 --> 00:12:42,802
[SPEAKER_01]: That's the difference, pretty much.

134
00:12:43,783 --> 00:12:54,953
[SPEAKER_01]: Because if you're describing that body that you see peri... like right now, while we speak, that body, that, you know, the mouth that's speaking, and the form behind, yeah, it's inaccessible, it exists there, and I'm using it to express these thoughts, and so on.

135
00:12:55,882 --> 00:13:04,067
[SPEAKER_01]: But if you're not aware of it peripherally, while we're speaking right here, right now, using this mouth and this form and so on, then it just becomes an explanation that then people can adopt.

136
00:13:04,527 --> 00:13:07,629
[SPEAKER_01]: But it does not pertain to that which it should pertain.

137
00:13:12,372 --> 00:13:13,712
[SPEAKER_01]: So, in other words, it's just abstract.

138
00:13:16,894 --> 00:13:24,759
[SPEAKER_01]: So, that's why, rather than trying to figure out these abstractions, you won't understand anicca,

139
00:13:26,558 --> 00:13:34,480
[SPEAKER_01]: Take on the precepts, guard the sense doors, you'll see why later on, and then start challenging things that are dear to you.

140
00:13:35,080 --> 00:13:42,462
[SPEAKER_01]: Not... you don't need to destroy them or get rid of them, just contemplate their cessation, their destruction, their change, being taken away.

141
00:13:43,823 --> 00:13:45,903
[SPEAKER_01]: And when the anxiety arises, fear... My health.

142
00:13:46,383 --> 00:13:47,744
[SPEAKER_01]: My health as well, yeah, sure.

143
00:13:47,824 --> 00:13:52,485
[SPEAKER_01]: Not just people that are dear to you and other things and items, your own body is a thing and item.

144
00:13:53,274 --> 00:13:55,495
[SPEAKER_01]: Your health, your life, everything that depends on.

145
00:13:56,175 --> 00:14:06,180
[SPEAKER_01]: And then, when anxiety starts to come up, and fear, and doubt, that's where you're going to see the benefit of previously developed precepts, guarding of the sense doors, and sense restraint.

146
00:14:06,260 --> 00:14:07,900
[SPEAKER_01]: That will keep together, in other words.

147
00:14:07,961 --> 00:14:12,342
[SPEAKER_01]: The aggregate of virtue developed beforehand, will be your container.

148
00:14:12,743 --> 00:14:22,627
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, yeah, that sense of self, it's going to start to rebel, and complain, and scream, and kick, because you're dissolving it, through challenging its ownership.

149
00:14:26,092 --> 00:14:30,075
[SPEAKER_01]: By developing that perception, by contemplating it.

150
00:14:30,155 --> 00:14:36,058
[SPEAKER_01]: By confining it within those perceptions.

151
00:14:36,078 --> 00:14:43,523
[SPEAKER_00]: And I think, when you start to speak about anicca, you can't help but describe dukkha.

152
00:14:44,563 --> 00:14:46,745
[SPEAKER_00]: Describe... Well, yeah, it's there.

153
00:14:46,965 --> 00:14:47,485
[SPEAKER_01]: It's there, yeah.

154
00:14:47,745 --> 00:14:48,586
[SPEAKER_01]: It sounds like the same.

155
00:14:49,286 --> 00:14:52,108
[SPEAKER_01]: It's just different aspects of the same thing, on the same throne.

156
00:14:52,808 --> 00:14:55,250
[SPEAKER_01]: Because, seeing that un-unability

157
00:14:59,002 --> 00:15:03,785
[SPEAKER_01]: Like, you know, simultaneously with the perception of anicca, it's a simultaneous dukkha.

158
00:15:04,625 --> 00:15:06,566
[SPEAKER_01]: It's not a second thing that follows later.

159
00:15:07,727 --> 00:15:09,508
[SPEAKER_01]: So it's the same facet.

160
00:15:09,668 --> 00:15:11,489
[SPEAKER_01]: It's the facet that's there at the same time.

161
00:15:12,790 --> 00:15:16,872
[SPEAKER_01]: And then the experience of that anicca-dukkha cannot be mind, cannot be atta.

162
00:15:18,873 --> 00:15:25,677
[SPEAKER_01]: So, on the level of self-awareness of anicca and dukkha, it's anatta.

163
00:15:28,581 --> 00:15:30,062
[SPEAKER_01]: You cannot own it, even if you want to.

164
00:15:30,142 --> 00:15:40,967
[SPEAKER_01]: Even if your sense of self is still trying to assume the ownership and rebel, if you confine it within the perception of anicca and dukkha, and endure it within anicca and dukkha, that sense of self is fading.

165
00:15:41,467 --> 00:15:43,008
[SPEAKER_01]: It is disintegrating.

166
00:15:43,028 --> 00:15:43,968
[SPEAKER_01]: It is wasting away.

167
00:15:45,229 --> 00:15:48,010
[SPEAKER_01]: Because it's unsustainable in that environment.

